          body:
            "A [[paraneoplastic-neurological-syndrome|paraneoplastic neurological syndrome]] (PNS) is nervous system damage caused by an immune response against a cancer, not by the tumor invading, compressing, or spreading to the nervous system, and not by treatment side effects. The tumor expresses a protein normally found in neurons. The immune system recognizes it as foreign, and the response that attacks the tumor also attacks neurons that carry the same protein.\n\nThe 2021 updated criteria replaced the old term \"classical syndromes\" with **high-risk phenotypes**: syndromes in which cancer is frequent enough that a search is always justified.\n\n1. [[encephalomyelitis|Encephalomyelitis]]: inflammation at several levels of the nervous system at once (for example limbic, brainstem, spinal cord, and dorsal root ganglia).\n2. [[limbic-encephalitis|Limbic encephalitis]]: subacute memory loss, confusion, and temporal lobe seizures, often with mesial temporal MRI change.\n3. Rapidly progressive [[paraneoplastic-cerebellar-degeneration|cerebellar syndrome]]: severe gait and limb [[ataxia|ataxia]], [[dysarthria|dysarthria]], and [[nystagmus|nystagmus]] developing over weeks to a few months.\n4. [[opsoclonus-myoclonus|Opsoclonus-myoclonus]]: chaotic, multidirectional rapid eye movements with [[myoclonus|myoclonus]] and ataxia; in children it is linked to neuroblastoma.\n5. Sensory [[neuronopathy|neuronopathy]]: loss of the [[dorsal-root-ganglion|dorsal root ganglion]] cell bodies, so the sensory loss is asymmetric and not length dependent, with [[sensory-ataxia|sensory ataxia]] and [[pseudoathetosis|pseudoathetosis]].\n6. [[gastrointestinal-pseudo-obstruction|Gastrointestinal pseudo-obstruction]] (enteric neuropathy): severe dysmotility with signs of bowel obstruction but no mechanical blockage.\n7. [[lambert-eaton-syndrome|Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome]] (LEMS): proximal leg weakness, reduced reflexes, and autonomic symptoms, with small cell lung cancer in about half of patients.\n\n**Intermediate-risk phenotypes** are syndromes that can be paraneoplastic but more often are not. Examples taught under this heading include some brainstem encephalitides, isolated myelopathies, stiff-person spectrum disorders, and polyradiculoneuropathies. Whether they are paraneoplastic depends heavily on the antibody found.\n\nThe tempo matters as much as the anatomy. Most PNS are subacute, worsening over days to a few months, and many patients are severely disabled by the time the syndrome peaks.",
