          body:
            "The 2021 criteria group antibodies by how often they come with cancer:\n\n- **High-risk antibodies** (cancer in more than 70%): Hu, CV2/CRMP5, SOX1, amphiphysin, Ri, Yo, Ma2, Tr (DNER), KLHL11, and PCA-2 (MAP1B). Most target intracellular proteins. These are the classic [[onconeural-antibody|onconeural antibodies]].\n- **Intermediate-risk antibodies** (cancer in 30% to 70%): for example AMPA receptor, GABA-B receptor, NMDA receptor (risk varies with age and sex), and P/Q-type [[voltage-gated-calcium-channel|voltage-gated calcium channel]] (VGCC) antibodies.\n- **Lower-risk antibodies**: for example LGI1, CASPR2, and GABA-A receptor antibodies, which only occasionally come with a tumor.\n\nIntracellular antigens are hidden from circulating antibody. The antibody is a marker of the immune response, and the damage is done mainly by cytotoxic T cells that kill neurons. Lost neurons do not come back, which is why high-risk antibody syndromes often respond poorly to immunotherapy and why finding and treating the tumor quickly matters. Surface antibodies (such as NMDAR or LGI1) bind the target directly, often cause reversible dysfunction, and respond better.\n\nMatching the antibody to the likely cancer focuses the search:\n\n- Hu (ANNA-1): [[small-cell-lung-cancer|small cell lung cancer]] in most adults; sensory neuronopathy, encephalomyelitis, limbic encephalitis, enteric neuropathy.\n- Yo (PCA-1): ovarian and other gynecological cancers, and breast cancer; rapidly progressive cerebellar syndrome in women.\n- Ri (ANNA-2): breast cancer and small cell lung cancer; brainstem syndromes and opsoclonus-myoclonus.\n- Ma2: testicular germ cell tumors in young men, lung and other cancers in older patients; limbic, diencephalic, and upper brainstem encephalitis.\n- KLHL11: testicular germ cell tumors, especially [[seminoma|seminoma]]; brainstem and cerebellar syndrome in men, often with hearing loss and tinnitus.\n- CV2/CRMP5: small cell lung cancer and thymoma; multifocal syndromes with chorea, optic neuropathy, myelopathy, and neuropathy.\n- Amphiphysin: breast cancer and small cell lung cancer; paraneoplastic stiff-person syndrome.\n- SOX1: small cell lung cancer, often with LEMS.\n- VGCC (P/Q type): LEMS, with small cell lung cancer in about half of patients.",
