          body:
            "**Specimen.** For most high-risk intracellular antibodies, serum testing is usually sufficient, and CSF can be added. CSF is more important for neural surface antibodies such as NMDAR, where serum-only testing can miss cases and produce false positives, so a suspected paraneoplastic encephalitis is best tested on serum and CSF together. Reference laboratories offer paired serum and CSF panels for this reason.\n\n**Method and line blot pitfalls.** Many laboratories screen with a commercial [[line-blot|line blot]] or immunoblot that has a strip for each intracellular antigen. These are convenient but give false positives, especially weak bands. The 2021 criteria recommend confirming intracellular antibody results with a [[tissue-based-assay|tissue-based assay]] (immunofluorescence or immunohistochemistry on brain tissue), which shows the expected staining pattern. A weak line blot band, with negative tissue staining, in a patient whose syndrome is not a typical PNS, is much more likely to be a [[false-positive|false positive]] than a real paraneoplastic antibody. Acting on it can lead to repeated whole-body imaging and invasive procedures for a cancer that is not there.\n\n**Screening.** Choose the search by phenotype and antibody:\n\n- CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis is the usual first step for most adults.\n- If CT is negative, [[fdg-pet|FDG-PET]] (usually combined with CT) can find small tumors, including mediastinal lymph node disease from small cell lung cancer.\n- Mammography, followed by breast MRI if needed, for Yo, Ri, and amphiphysin in women.\n- Pelvic (transvaginal) ultrasound, with CT or MRI, for Yo and for NMDAR encephalitis in women.\n- Testicular ultrasound for Ma2 and KLHL11 in men.\n\n**Repeat screening.** If the first search is negative and the suspicion remains, screen again. The EFNS task force advised repeating screening after 3 to 6 months and then every 6 months for up to 4 years, and for LEMS, screening for 2 years was considered enough. A first negative scan in a patient with a high-risk antibody means the tumor has not been found yet.",
